//RS /// #2027 criterion 3: one signal mechanism means one observable answer.
//RS ///
//RS /// The same program, the same real `SIGINT`, on all three tiers — AOT, default
//RS /// `jet run` (resident Cranelift) and `jet run --interpret`. The assertion is
//RS /// cross-tier EQUALITY first, then the pinned value, because the failure this
//RS /// guards against is precisely two tiers disagreeing about a delivered signal.
//RS ///
//RS /// Only the unified part is asserted: that the interrupt is counted once, and
//RS /// that both handlers run in registration order. A handler's *failure* shape is
//RS /// the one thing that is still per-tier by design (AOT catches the unwind at the
//RS /// dispatcher boundary, the resident host sets a runtime trap, the evaluator
//RS /// returns a diagnostic), so no handler here fails.
//RS #[cfg(unix)]
//RS #[test]
//RS fn core_os_interrupt_delivery_matches_aot_jit_and_interpreter() {
//RS     use std::io::{BufRead, Read};
